      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's consider the original equation:\n$$(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ).$$\nTo expand this, we apply the distributive property:\n$$(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)[1 + \\tan 25^\\circ] = 1 \\cdot [1 + \\tan 25^\\circ] + \\tan 20^\\circ \\cdot [1 + \\tan 25^\\circ]$$\nThis further simplifies to:\n$$1 \\cdot [1 + \\tan 25^\\circ] + \\tan 20^\\circ \\cdot [1 + \\tan 25^\\circ] = 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ.$$\n\nNext, we use the identity $\\tan 25^\\circ = \\cot 65^\\circ.$ Applying this to our expression, we obtain:\n$$1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan (90^\\circ - 25^\\circ) + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan (90^\\circ - 25^\\circ)$$\n$$= 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\cot 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\cot 25^\\circ.$$\n\nNow, we change $\\cot 25^\\circ$ into $\\frac{1}{\\tan 25^\\circ}.$ Then the expression becomes:\n$$1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\frac{1}{\\tan 25^\\circ} + \\tan 20^\\circ \\cot 25^\\circ.$$\n\nWe know that $\\tan 20^\\circ \\cot 25^\\circ = 1$ from the trigonometric identity $\\cot \\theta \\tan \\theta = 1$ for $\\theta \\neq 0^\\circ.$ The identity applies here because both 20 and 25 are positive angles. Thus, we simplify the expression to:\n$$1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\frac{1}{\\tan 25^\\circ} + 1.$$\n\nCalling the original expression $A,$ we see that:\n$$A = \\frac{2}{\\tan 25^\\circ} + \\frac{2}{\\tan 25^\\circ} = \\frac{4}{\\tan 25^\\circ}.$$\n\nSince $1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\frac{4}{\\tan 25^\\circ},$ we know that:\n$$\\frac{A}{2} = \\sec 22.5^\\circ.$$\n\nSince $\\sec 22.5^\\circ = \\sqrt{2} + 1,$\n$$ \\boxed{{(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)} = \\sqrt{2} + 1}. $$\n\nThis problem can be solved using the sine and tangent angle sum formulas and the sum of angles formula for tangent.\n1. $\\tan 20^\\circ$ and $\\sin 22.5^\\circ$ are opposites of each other in the $$\\triangle T  group, which is proven by Shaping up the angles in the triangle and substituting them. \n2. Intermediate interim calculation, $\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = 1.$\n3. Combining results from (1) and (2), the result of the original expression is $$\\boxed{\\frac{2}{\\tan 25^\\circ}}.$$",
